1. # CARE's data fetching utilities
2. 
3. CARE now uses TanStack Query (formerly React Query) as its data fetching solution.
4. 
5. ## Using TanStack Query (Recommended for new code)
6. 
7. For new API integrations, we recommend using TanStack Query with `query` utility function. This is a wrapper around `fetch` that works seamlessly with TanStack Query. It handles response parsing, error handling, setting headers, and more.
8. 
9. ````tsx
10. import { useQuery } from "@tanstack/react-query";
11. import query from "@/Utils/request/query";
12. 
13. export default function UserProfile() {
14.   const { data, isLoading } = useQuery({
15.     queryKey: [routes.users.current.path],
16.     queryFn: query(routes.users.current)
17.   });
18. 
19.   if (isLoading) return <Loading />;
20.   return <div>{data?.name}</div>;
21. }
22. 
23. // With path parameters
24. function PatientDetails({ id }: { id: string }) {
25.   const { data } = useQuery({
26.     queryKey: ['patient', id],
27.     queryFn: query(patientApi.get, {
28.       pathParams: { id }
29.     })
30.   });
31. 
32.   return <div>{data?.name}</div>;
33. }
34. 
35. // With query parameters
36. function SearchMedicines() {
37.   const { data } = useQuery({
38.     queryKey: ['medicines', 'paracetamol'],
39.     queryFn: query(routes.medicine.search, {
40.       queryParams: { search: 'paracetamol' }
41.     })
42.   });
43. 
44.   return <MedicinesList medicines={data?.results} />;
45. }
46. 
47. // When you need response status/error handling
48. function FacilityDetails({ id }: { id: string }) {
49.   const { data, isLoading } = useQuery({
50.     queryKey: ["facility", id],
51.     queryFn: query(routes.getFacility, {
52.       pathParams: { id },
53.       silent: true
54.     })
55.   });
56. 
57.   if (isLoading) return <Loading />;
58.   return <div>{data?.name}</div>;
59. }
60. 
61. ### query
62. 
63. `query` is our wrapper around fetch that works seamlessly with TanStack Query. It:
64. - Handles response parsing (JSON, text, blobs).
65. - Constructs proper error objects.
66. - Sets the headers appropriately.
67. - Integrates with our global error handling.
68. 
69. ```typescript
70. interface APICallOptions {
71.   pathParams?: Record<string, string>;  // URL parameters
72.   queryParams?: QueryParams;            // Query string parameters
73.   body?: TBody;                         // Request body
74.   silent?: boolean;                     // Suppress error notifications
75.   headers?: HeadersInit;                // Additional headers
76. }
77. 
78. // Basic usage
79. useQuery({
80.   queryKey: ["users"],
81.   queryFn: query(routes.users.list)
82. });
83. 
84. // With parameters
85. useQuery({
86.   queryKey: ["user", id],
87.   queryFn: query(routes.users.get, {
88.     pathParams: { id },
89.     queryParams: { include: "details" },
90.     silent: true  // Optional: suppress error notifications
91.   })
92. });
93. ````
94. 
95. ### Debounced Queries
96. 
97. For search inputs or other scenarios requiring debounced API calls, use `query.debounced`:
98. 
99. ```tsx
100. function SearchComponent() {
101.   const [search, setSearch] = useState("");
102. 
103.   const { data } = useQuery({
104.     queryKey: ["search", search],
105.     queryFn: query.debounced(routes.search, {
106.       queryParams: { q: search },
107.       debounceInterval: 500, // Optional: defaults to 500ms
108.     }),
109.     enabled: search.length > 0,
110.   });
111. 
112.   return (
113.     <Input
114.       value={search}
115.       onChange={(e) => setSearch(e.target.value)}
116.       placeholder="Search..."
117.     />
118.   );
119. }
120. ```
121. 
122. The debounced query will wait for the specified interval after the last call before executing the request, helping to reduce unnecessary API calls during rapid user input.
123. 
124. ### Error Handling
125. 
126. All API errors are now handled globally. Common scenarios like:
127. 
128. - Session expiry -> Redirects to /session-expired
129. - Bad requests (400/406) -> Shows error notification
130.   are automatically handled.
131. 
132. Use the `silent: true` option to suppress error notifications for specific queries.
133. 
134. ## Using Mutations with TanStack Query
135. 
136. For data mutations, we provide a `mutate` utility that works seamlessly with TanStack Query's `useMutation` hook.
137. 
138. ```tsx
139. import { useMutation } from "@tanstack/react-query";
140. 
141. import mutate from "@/Utils/request/mutate";
142. 
143. function CreatePrescription({ consultationId }: { consultationId: string }) {
144.   const { mutate: createPrescription, isPending } = useMutation({
145.     mutationFn: mutate(MedicineRoutes.createPrescription, {
146.       pathParams: { consultationId },
147.     }),
148.     onSuccess: () => {
149.       toast.success("Prescription created successfully");
150.     },
151.   });
152. 
153.   return (
154.     <Button
155.       onClick={() =>
156.         createPrescription({ medicineId: "123", dosage: "1x daily" })
157.       }
158.       disabled={isPending}
159.     >
160.       Create Prescription
161.     </Button>
162.   );
163. }
164. 
165. // With path parameters and complex payload
166. function UpdatePatient({ patientId }: { patientId: string }) {
167.   const { mutate: updatePatient } = useMutation({
168.     mutationFn: mutate(PatientRoutes.update, {
169.       pathParams: { id: patientId },
170.       silent: true, // Optional: suppress error notifications
171.     }),
172.   });
173. 
174.   const handleSubmit = (data: PatientData) => {
175.     updatePatient(data);
176.   };
177. 
178.   return <PatientForm onSubmit={handleSubmit} />;
179. }
180. ```
181.

